 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Polarity",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "An A-suffix part is unidirectional and has a cathode band. A CA-suffix part is bidirectional with no band; it carries the same figures as the A part of the same voltage, but where VR is 10 V or less its IR limit is doubled. Forward voltage and forward surge (Fig. 6) apply to unidirectional parts only."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Pulse rating",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "600 W is the single-pulse rating for the 10/1000 µs shape: the current rises to its peak in 10 µs and has decayed to half the peak by 1 ms (Fig. 3). VC in the table is each part's maximum clamping voltage at its own IPP with that pulse. The allowed peak power rises for shorter pulses and falls for longer ones, from about 30 kW at 0.1 µs to about 0.5 kW at 1000 µs (Fig. 1)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Derating",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "Peak pulse power or current is 100 % up to Ta = 25 ℃, then falls in a straight line to zero at 150 ℃ (Fig. 2). Steady dissipation PM(AV) is 5.0 W up to 50 ℃, then falls in a straight line to zero at 150 ℃ (Fig. 5)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Forward surge",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "A unidirectional part withstands 100 A for a single 8.3 ms half sine starting at TJ max; with repeated 60 Hz cycles the permitted peak drops, to about 24 A at 100 cycles (Fig. 6)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Leakage and capacitance",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Typical IR is under 5 µA for parts above 12 V. The table maximum is 5 µA from SMBJ9.0A upward and rises to 800 µA for the lowest-voltage parts. Junction capacitance drops as breakdown voltage rises and as reverse bias is applied: at zero bias, about 3000 pF (unidirectional) at 5 V falling to about 50 pF at 450 V (Fig. 4)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Construction and failure",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Glass-passivated junction; plastic package flammability rated UL 94V-0; matte tin lead-free plating; halogen free and RoHS compliant. The terminals take 260 ℃ for 40 s during soldering. Whisker testing is to JEDEC JESD201A, tables 4a and 4c. Over-voltage or over-current typically leaves the part shorted."
  }
 ],
